<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Penghu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pescadores</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Penghu, also called the Pescadores, is an archipelago of about 90 small islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan-strait">Taiwan Strait</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> occupied it from 1622 until a Ming fleet forced them to withdraw to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1624. In 1683 the Qing admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-lang">Shi Lang</a> defeated the Zheng fleet there, and in 1895 the islands were ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> with Taiwan; they are now governed by the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/penghu.jpg" alt="Penghu" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sleepingstar · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Penghu_County_Montage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Republic of China today governs Taiwan, <b>Penghu</b>, Kinmen and Matsu, with about 23 million people, and is formally recognised by only a small number of states, while the People’s Republic claims the island and has not renounced the use of force.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Shimonoseki China recognised the independence of Korea, ceded Taiwan, the <b>Penghu</b> Islands and the Liaodong Peninsula, which the Triple Intervention soon forced Japan to return, and agreed to pay an indemnity of 200 million taels.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>China recognised Korean independence, ceded Taiwan and the <b>Pescadores</b> to Japan and paid a large indemnity.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-17">17 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Seeking a base for the China trade, the Dutch East India Company failed to take Macau in 1622 and occupied the <b>Penghu</b> islands, but a Ming fleet forced them out in 1624.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-formosa">Dutch Formosa</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1683 the Qing admiral Shi Lang destroyed the Zheng fleet at <b>Penghu</b>, and Zheng Keshuang surrendered.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tungning">Kingdom of Tungning</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qing Empire ceded Taiwan and the <b>Penghu</b> islands to Japan by the Treaty of Shimonoseki in 1895.</blockquote><a href="/state/taiwan-under-japanese-rule">Taiwan under Japanese rule</a></li></ul></section>
